    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d279">

                <pb facs="1048" n="1009" xml:id="pg1009"/>

                <head>279. Memorandum From Michel Oksenberg
                    of the National Security Council Staff to the President’s Assistant for National
                    Security Affairs (Brzezinski)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d279-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1979-10-17">October 17, 1979</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>Human Rights in China Heats Up</item>
                </list>
                <p>A PRC dissident has been sentenced to 15 years
                    for passing secrets to foreign journalists, among other things.</p>
                <p>Fox Butterfield, of the <hi rendition="#italic">Times,</hi> is likely to make him
                    a human rights martyr, and sentencing may be a topic of a <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Times</hi> editorial.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d279-f2"/>
                    One question being raised is whether stiff sentences in China will lead to
                    restrictions on computer sales to the PRC.</p>
                <p>State will issue on an “if-asked” basis a statement decrying the stiff sentence.
                    This is likely to elicit negative Chinese reaction, but it is probably better to
                    go that way than to be accused of applying obvious dual standards. I will try to
                    remain on top of this issue.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d279-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d279-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, National Security Affairs, Brzezinski Material, Country File, Box 9, China (PRC): 10/79–2/80. Confidential. Sent for
                    information. A stamped notation indicates that Brzezinski saw the memorandum.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d279-f2" n="2" xml:id="d279-f2-content">
                <p>Butterfield wrote two articles about Wei Jinsheng for <hi rendition="#italic">The
                        New York Times:</hi> the first, “4 Arrested in China at Democracy Wall,” on
                    November 12, p. A7; the second, “China Dissident Likens Present to the
                    Repressive Past,” on November 15, p. A22. There were no editorials in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">The New York Times</hi> about Wei during this
                    period.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
